#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110391 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "69d6ed5b-1c29-53a7-a37e-817042a02861" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/id #uuid "5e74c09c-ebb2-56f2-98f1-953e6e7e3fcc" :question/math-academy-id "e-3708" :question/problem "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y=\\arcsin x$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1483/Source/Images/1539fbd9280645796c80ede384464105.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of\n\n$$\ny=\\arcsin x\n$$\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1, 1]$.\n- The range of the function is $y\\in\\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}2, \\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right]$.\n- The minimum value occurs at $x=-1$.\n- The maximum value occurs at $x=1$.\n- The zero occurs at $x=0$.\n- The function is strictly increasing on its entire domain.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is III."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110392 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e88f43c-18c5-5fdb-b6d1-579370489099" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Domain of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/id #uuid "62d7cf82-1892-5bf2-96f8-5e2f0b76e5bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-3714" :question/problem "Which of the following values is in the domain of $y=\\arcsin x$?\n\n1. $-3$\n2. $0$\n3. $2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arcsin x\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1483/Source/Images/5eb0444f42978e37f9a0aff28a462452.png)\n\nNote that the domain of the function is\n\n$$\nx=\\left[ -1, 1\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value $x=0$ lies in the domain of the\n\n$$\ny=\\arcsin x\n$$\n\nwhile the values $x=-3$ and $x=2$ do not."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110393 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "76b0ad1e-827e-5795-8431-3276c8eed881" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Range of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/id #uuid "04b531a3-9e17-58b0-b25f-e09e6ddf0bdb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3715" :question/problem "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\arcsin x$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac \\pi 2$\n2. $\\pi$\n3. $-2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arcsin x\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1483/Source/Images/6abddc7d6e11d924bf1c930548c02c35.png)\n\nNote that the range of the function is\n\n$$\ny\\in\\left[-\\dfrac\\pi 2, \\dfrac\\pi 2\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nlies in the range of\n\n$$\ny=\\arcsin x\n$$\n\nwhile the values $x=\\pi$ and $x=-2$ do not."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110394 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67692569-e3a4-51e8-99ac-79d8b3081363" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/id #uuid "aec97277-ccea-523c-b84e-cce4857b6bfd" :question/math-academy-id "e-3716" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\arcsin x$?\n\n1. $\\left(\\dfrac 1 2,\\dfrac {\\pi}{2}\\right)$\n2. $\\left(0,\\pi\\right)$\n3. $\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function\n\n$$\ny=\\arcsin x\n$$\n\nthe following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\sin x$.\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\arcsin x$, which is $y\\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.\n\n- First, we consider the point $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$. We note the following:The point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\sin x$. $\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$ Therefore, the point $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\arcsin x$.\n\n- Next, we consider the point $\\left(0,\\pi \\right)$. We note the following:The point $\\left(\\pi,0\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\sin x.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$The range of $y=\\arcsin x$ is $y\\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$, and $y=\\pi$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$ Therefore, the point $\\left(0,\\pi\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\arcsin x$.\n- Finally, we consider the point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$. We note the following:The point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\sin x.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$ The range of $y=\\arcsin x$ is $y\\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$, and $y=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ lies within this range. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$ Therefore, the point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\arcsin x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111094 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "69d6ed5b-1c29-53a7-a37e-817042a02861" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85251810-38ab-5c23-8000-832183eefbba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1483/Source/Images/q-42117-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108660} {:answer/id #uuid "abdfb5b0-c85f-5799-b427-6af8ee1ed617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1483/Source/Images/q-42117-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108661} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd132f0-d5bb-5367-b1a4-75bd6fb41b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1483/Source/Images/q-42117-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108662} {:answer/id #uuid "2994ee93-1150-5d4d-8ef2-03630d797e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/va